{"a":"DDInter1164","b":"DDInter1707","level":"Moderate","mech":["Synergy"],"description":"Diuretic-induced renal impairment and dehydration may increase the risk of lactic acidosis in patients who are concomitantly taking metformin. In addition, thiazides and other diuretics may interfere with glucose control by causing hyperglycemia, glucose intolerance, new-onset diabetes mellitus, and/or exacerbation of preexisting diabetes. abdominal pain acute pancreatitis adjustment disorder respiratory alkalosis anaemia angina Anxiety aortic stenosis Arachnoiditis arterial insufficiency More","management":"Close clinical monitoring is recommended if diuretics are coadministered with antidiabetic agents. Patients should be advised to monitor their blood glucose and to promptly notify their doctor if they experience possible signs of lactic acidosis (such as malaise, myalgia, respiratory distress, hyperventilation, slow or irregular heartbeat, somnolence, abdominal upset) or loss of glycemic control. Dose adjustments of metformin may be required. Likewise, patients should be observed for hypoglycemia if diuretics are withdrawn from their therapeutic regimen.","iid":965527},
{"a":"DDInter1164","b":"DDInter1791","level":"Unknown","mech":[],"description":"abdominal pain aphasia Aching joints ascites back injury brain concussion Breast disorder bronchitis bulimia Candida Infection More","management":"-","iid":1146823},
{"a":"DDInter1164","b":"DDInter1874","level":"Moderate","mech":["Excretion"],"description":"Trimethoprim may decrease the excretion of metformin by competing for renal tubular transport. The mechanism may involve trimethoprim inhibition of the organic cation transporter 2 (OCT2), thereby increasing metformin plasma concentration. Increased metformin levels may increase the risk of lactic acidosis. transient ischaemic attack cholecystectomies dermatitis exfoliative Mod Fatigue gall bladder hepatitis hernia Excess potassium arthropathy More","management":"If trimethoprim and metformin must be used together, particularly slow and cautious titration of metformin dosage is recommended. The maximal dose of metformin probably also should be reduced until further information about this interaction is available. Patients should be advised to monitor their blood glucose and to promptly notify their physician if they experience possible signs of lactic acidosis such as malaise, myalgia, respiratory distress, hyperventilation, slow or irregular heartbeat, somnolence, abdominal upset, or other unusual symptoms.","iid":965548},
{"a":"DDInter1164","b":"DDInter1928","level":"Moderate","mech":["Excretion"],"description":"Verapamil may decrease the glucose-lowering effects of metformin. The mechanism may involve verapamil inhibition of the organic cation transporter 1 (OCT1), thereby inhibiting hepatic uptake of metformin. abdominal pain abscess allergic rhinitis alopecia Amnesia anaphylactic reaction angina angioedema Anxiety aortic aneurysm More","management":"Caution is advised if verapamil and metformin are used concomitantly. Close clinical monitoring of glycemic control is recommended following initiation or discontinuation of these drugs, and the dosages may be adjusted as necessary.","iid":965556},
