{"a":"DDInter1202","b":"DDInter1675","level":"Moderate","mech":["Synergy"],"description":"The risk of peripheral neuropathy may be increased during concurrent use of two or more agents that are associated with this adverse effect. Patient risk factors include diabetes and age older than 60 years. abdominal distension abdominal pain Acidosis renal tubular acidosis Amnesia anaphylactic reaction Anorexia Anxiety aortic aneurysm asthenia More","management":"Patients should be monitored closely for symptoms of neuropathy such as burning, tingling, pain, or numbness in the hands and feet. Since the development of peripheral neuropathy may be dose-related for many drugs, the recommended dosages should generally not be exceeded. Consideration should be given to dosage reduction or immediate discontinuation of these medications in patients who develop peripheral neuropathy to limit further damage.","iid":992155},
