{"a":"DDInter1291","b":"DDInter20","level":"Moderate","mech":["Antagonism"],"description":"Limited data indicate that some cyclooxygenase inhibitors may attenuate the antihypertensive effects of some calcium channel blockers. The mechanism appears to be related to an alteration of vascular tone, which is dependent on prostacyclins and other vasodilatory prostanoids. When a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID) is added to the regimen of a patient who is already taking a calcium channel blocker, increased blood pressure may result. Also, the clinician should be aware that the risk of hypotension is increased when NSAIDs are withdrawn from the regimen. abdominal pain Acidosis anaemia AFIB cerebral infarct chest pain arteriosclerotic heart disease gastroenteritis gastrointestinal bleed hyperglycaemia More","management":"Monitoring for altered blood pressure control is recommended.","iid":955962},
{"a":"DDInter1291","b":"DDInter271","level":"Moderate","mech":["Antagonism"],"description":"Calcium-containing products may decrease the effectiveness of calcium channel blockers by saturating calcium channels with calcium. Calcium chloride has been used to manage acute severe verapamil toxicity.","management":"Management consists of monitoring the effectiveness of calcium channel blocker therapy during coadministration with calcium products.","iid":955999},
{"a":"DDInter1291","b":"DDInter48","level":"Unknown","mech":[],"description":"acquired immune deficiency syndrome alcohol consumption anaphylactic reaction haemolytic anaemia Prinzmetal variant angina angioedema Anorexia atelectasis Atherosclerosis Bacteraemia More","management":"-","iid":1177633},
{"a":"DDInter1291","b":"DDInter513","level":"Moderate","mech":["Metabolism","Antagonism"],"description":"Corticosteroids may antagonize the effects of antihypertensive medications by inducing sodium and fluid retention. These effects may be more common with the natural corticosteroids (cortisone, hydrocortisone) because they have greater mineralocorticoid activity. Conversely, some calcium channel blockers such as diltiazem and verapamil may increase corticosteroid plasma levels and effects by inhibiting their clearance via CYP450 3A4 metabolism. birth defect abscess Acidosis agranulocytoses respiratory alkalosis haemolytic anaemia aortic regurgitation Arrhythmia asthma atelectasis More","management":"Patients on prolonged (i.e., longer than about a week) or high-dose corticosteroid therapy should have blood pressure, electrolyte levels, and body weight monitored regularly, and be observed for the development of edema and congestive heart failure. The dosages of antihypertensive medications may require adjustment.","iid":956053},
{"a":"DDInter1291","b":"DDInter537","level":"Moderate","mech":["Antagonism"],"description":"Limited data indicate that some cyclooxygenase inhibitors may attenuate the antihypertensive effects of some calcium channel blockers. The mechanism appears to be related to an alteration of vascular tone, which is dependent on prostacyclins and other vasodilatory prostanoids. When a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID) is added to the regimen of a patient who is already taking a calcium channel blocker, increased blood pressure may result. Also, the clinician should be aware that the risk of hypotension is increased when NSAIDs are withdrawn from the regimen. abdominal distension abortion spontaneous premature separation of placenta als anaemia aortic aneurysm Apnea appendectomy Arthritis bacterial Atherosclerosis More","management":"Monitoring for altered blood pressure control is recommended.","iid":956056},
{"a":"DDInter1291","b":"DDInter543","level":"Unknown","mech":[],"description":"anaemia Arrhythmia chest pain constipated arteriosclerotic heart disease Cough Diabetes Difficulty breathing fatty liver folate deficiency More","management":"-","iid":1166296},
{"a":"DDInter1291","b":"DDInter562","level":"Moderate","mech":["Synergy"],"description":"Many psychotherapeutic and CNS-active agents (e.g., anxiolytics, sedatives, hypnotics, antidepressants, antipsychotics, opioids, alcohol, muscle relaxants) exhibit hypotensive effects, especially during initiation of therapy and dose escalation. Coadministration with antihypertensives and other hypotensive agents, in particular vasodilators and alpha-blockers, may result in additive effects on blood pressure and orthostasis.","management":"Caution and close monitoring for development of hypotension is advised during coadministration of these agents. Avoid alcohol in patients receiving vasodilating antihypertensive drugs. Patients should be advised to avoid rising abruptly from a sitting or recumbent position and to notify their physician if they experience dizziness, lightheadedness, syncope, orthostasis, or tachycardia.","iid":956061},
{"a":"DDInter1291","b":"DDInter592","level":"Moderate","mech":["Synergy"],"description":"The hypotensive effects of nifedipine and alpha-adrenergic blockers may be additive. The proposed mechanism is additive vasodilatory effects. Postural hypotension may occur. abdominal distension agranulocytoses alcohol consumption Amnesia anaphylactic reaction aortic regurgitation arteriosclerosis Astrocytoma Atherosclerosis Bacterial infection More","management":"Hemodynamic responses should be monitored during coadministration, especially during the first few weeks of therapy. Patients should be advised to take the alpha-blocker at bedtime and to notify their physician if they experience dizziness or syncope while awake.","iid":956069},
{"a":"DDInter1291","b":"DDInter638","level":"Minor","mech":["Synergy"],"description":"Calcium channel blockers and angiotensin converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitors may have additive hypotensive effects. While these drugs are often safely used together, careful monitoring of the systemic blood pressure is recommended during coadministration, especially during the first one to three weeks of therapy. abdominal distension Alcoholic intoxication respiratory alkalosis Amnesia Anemia aplastic angioedema aortic regurgitation aortic stenosis aphasia Arrhythmia More","management":"-","iid":956082},
{"a":"DDInter1291","b":"DDInter652","level":"Unknown","mech":[],"description":"abdominal pain anaemia angioedema aortic aneurysm Arrhythmia Atherosclerosis AFIB Bacteraemia confusion constipated More","management":"-","iid":1161643},
{"a":"DDInter1291","b":"DDInter670","level":"Moderate","mech":["Metabolism"],"description":"An interaction between clarithromycin and nifedipine was suspected in a case of vasodilatory shock. The proposed mechanism is clarithromycin inhibition of nifedipine metabolism via CYP450 3A4, resulting in increased plasma levels and pharmacologic effects of nifedipine. abdominal pain Amnesia anaemia Anorexia aortic aneurysm atrioventricular block Bacteraemia bleb Candida Infection carpal tunnel More","management":"Caution is advised if nifedipine is prescribed with macrolides that inhibit CYP450 3A4. Pharmacologic response to nifedipine should be monitored more closely whenever a macrolide such as clarithromycin or erythromycin is added to or withdrawn from therapy, and the nifedipine dosage adjusted as necessary. Patients should be advised to contact their physician if they experience excessive adverse effects of nifedipine such as hypotension (as indicated by dizziness, lightheadedness, or fainting), irregular heart beats, peripheral edema, and dyspnea.","iid":956092},
