{"a":"DDInter79","b":"DDInter900","level":"Moderate","mech":["Antagonism"],"description":"Limited data indicate that some cyclooxygenase inhibitors may attenuate the antihypertensive effects of some calcium channel blockers. The mechanism appears to be related to an alteration of vascular tone, which is dependent on prostacyclins and other vasodilatory prostanoids. When a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID) is added to the regimen of a patient who is already taking a calcium channel blocker, increased blood pressure may result. Also, the clinician should be aware that the risk of hypotension is increased when NSAIDs are withdrawn from the regimen. abdominal pain Acidosis acute pancreatitis anaemia angina Anorexia Anxiety asthenia AFIB Back Ache More","management":"Monitoring for altered blood pressure control is recommended.","iid":957488},
